@page "/components/spacer"

<PageOutlet Url="components/spacer"
            Title="Spacer"
            Description="BitSpacer generates space between components: a flexible space that absorbs the leftover room of a flex container, or a fixed space in pixels, any CSS length, or the spacing scale of the theme." />

<DemoPage Name="Spacer"
          Description="BitSpacer generates space between components inside a flex container. Left alone it is flexible and pushes its neighbours apart; given Width, Height, Gap or Size it becomes a fixed space. It renders one empty element, hidden from assistive technologies."
          Parameters="componentParameters"
          CssVariables="componentCssVariables"
          SubEnums="componentSubEnums"
          GitHubUrl="Layouts/Spacer/BitSpacer.cs"
          GitHubDemoUrl="Layouts/Spacer/BitSpacerDemo.razor">
    <DemoExample Title="Basic" RazorCode="@example1RazorCode" Id="example1">
        <div class="example-desc">
            With no parameters the spacer is flexible: it takes the room the other children do not need, so one spacer pushes its neighbours to the two ends and several split the room evenly. It is a real flex child, so a container <code>gap</code> is added on both sides of it.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <div class="demo-row">
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Text" IconName="@BitIconName.GlobalNavButton" />
                <BitSpacer />
                <BitText Typography="BitTypography.H6">Title</BitText>
                <BitSpacer />
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Text" IconName="@BitIconName.Contact" />
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="demo-row">
                <div class="demo-box">Start</div>
                <BitSpacer />
                <div class="demo-box">End</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
    <DemoExample Title="Fixed size" RazorCode="@example2RazorCode" Id="example2">
        <div class="example-desc">
            <b>Width</b> is a fixed gap in pixels, <b>Gap</b> the same in any CSS length (<code>1rem</code>, <code>5%</code>, <code>var(--my-gap)</code>). A fixed spacer does not grow, and its logical margin never shrinks in a cramped container.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <div class="demo-row">
                <BitProgress Circular Indeterminate />
                <BitSpacer Width="64" />
                <BitProgress Circular Indeterminate />
                <BitSpacer Width="64" />
                <BitProgress Circular Indeterminate />
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="demo-row">
                <div class="demo-box">1rem</div>
                <BitSpacer Gap="1rem" />
                <div class="demo-box">4ch</div>
                <BitSpacer Gap="4ch" />
                <div class="demo-box">10%</div>
                <BitSpacer Gap="10%" />
                <div class="demo-box">end</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
    <DemoExample Title="Vertical" RazorCode="@example3RazorCode" Id="example3">
        <div class="example-desc">
            <b>Height</b> is the pixel gap of a column layout, and <b>Vertical</b> moves <b>Gap</b>, <b>Size</b> and <b>MinGap</b> to the block axis. A flexible spacer needs neither: it always grows along the main axis, pinning the last item to the bottom.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <div class="demo-col">
                <div class="demo-box">Height 8</div>
                <BitSpacer Height="8" />
                <div class="demo-box">Height 32</div>
                <BitSpacer Height="32" />
                <div class="demo-box">Vertical Gap 3rem</div>
                <BitSpacer Vertical Gap="3rem" />
                <div class="demo-box">Flexible</div>
                <BitSpacer />
                <div class="demo-box">Bottom</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
    <DemoExample Title="Grow" RazorCode="@example4RazorCode" Id="example4">
        <div class="example-desc">
            <b>Grow</b> is the flex-grow factor (default <b>1</b>). Several spacers split the leftover room in proportion to it: <b>1</b> and <b>2</b> give a one third / two thirds split.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <div class="demo-row">
                <div class="demo-box">A</div>
                <BitSpacer Grow="1" />
                <div class="demo-box">B</div>
                <BitSpacer Grow="2" />
                <div class="demo-box">C</div>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="demo-row">
                <div class="demo-box">A</div>
                <BitSpacer Grow="3" />
                <div class="demo-box">B</div>
                <BitSpacer Grow="1" />
                <div class="demo-box">C</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
    <DemoExample Title="Minimum size" RazorCode="@example5RazorCode" Id="example5">
        <div class="example-desc">
            <b>MinGap</b> is the floor of a flexible spacer, so its neighbours never touch. Drag the corner of each box to squeeze it: the first spacer keeps its <code>2rem</code>, the second collapses. A fixed spacer ignores it.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <div class="demo-row demo-resizable">
                <div class="demo-box">MinGap</div>
                <BitSpacer MinGap="2rem" />
                <div class="demo-box">2rem</div>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="demo-row demo-resizable">
                <div class="demo-box">No</div>
                <BitSpacer />
                <div class="demo-box">MinGap</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
    <DemoExample Title="Element" RazorCode="@example6RazorCode" Id="example6">
        <div class="example-desc">
            <b>Element</b> swaps the rendered <code>div</code> for another tag, such as an <code>li</code> in a list-based toolbar or menu, which only accepts list items.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <ul class="demo-row demo-list">
                <li class="demo-box">Home</li>
                <li class="demo-box">Products</li>
                <BitSpacer Element="li" />
                <li class="demo-box">Sign in</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
    <DemoExample Title="Accessibility" RazorCode="@example7RazorCode" Id="example7">
        <div class="example-desc">
            A spacer is <code>aria-hidden</code> by default, so screen readers skip the empty box. Give it an
            <b>AriaLabel</b> when the gap marks a boundary worth announcing: it then renders as a named
            <code>separator</code>. An explicit <code>role</code> or <code>aria-hidden</code> attribute still wins.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <div class="demo-row" role="toolbar" aria-label="Document">
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Text" IconName="@BitIconName.Save" AriaLabel="Save" />
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Text" IconName="@BitIconName.Print" AriaLabel="Print" />
                <BitSpacer AriaLabel="Support" />
                <BitButton Variant="BitVariant.Text" IconName="@BitIconName.Help" AriaLabel="Help" />
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
    <DemoExample Title="Cascading parameters" RazorCode="@example8RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example8CsharpCode" Id="example8">
        <div class="example-desc">
            <b>BitParams</b> hands a <b>BitSpacerParams</b> to every spacer under it, so a toolbar or a page sets its
            rhythm once. The values are defaults, not overrides: a spacer that sets a parameter itself keeps its own.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <BitParams Parameters="@spacerParams">
                <div class="demo-row">
                    <div class="demo-box">Cascaded</div>
                    <BitSpacer />
                    <div class="demo-box">1.5rem</div>
                    <BitSpacer />
                    <div class="demo-box">Own Gap</div>
                    <BitSpacer Gap="4rem" />
                    <div class="demo-box">4rem</div>
                </div>
            </BitParams>
            <br />
            <div class="demo-row">
                <div class="demo-box">Outside</div>
                <BitSpacer />
                <div class="demo-box">Flexible again</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
    <DemoExample Title="Size" RazorCode="@example9RazorCode" Id="example9">
        <div class="example-desc">
            <b>Size</b> takes the fixed space from the theme's spacing scale, so it follows the theme's spacing unit and density. It follows <b>Vertical</b>; <b>Gap</b>, <b>Width</b> and <b>Height</b> win over it. Each step is a public CSS variable: the last row re-sizes <b>Large</b> with <code>--bit-Spacer-large-size</code>.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <div class="demo-row">
                <div class="demo-box">Small</div>
                <BitSpacer Size="BitSize.Small" />
                <div class="demo-box">Medium</div>
                <BitSpacer Size="BitSize.Medium" />
                <div class="demo-box">Large</div>
                <BitSpacer Size="BitSize.Large" />
                <div class="demo-box">End</div>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="demo-col demo-col-auto">
                <div class="demo-box">Vertical Small</div>
                <BitSpacer Vertical Size="BitSize.Small" />
                <div class="demo-box">Vertical Large</div>
                <BitSpacer Vertical Size="BitSize.Large" />
                <div class="demo-box">End</div>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="demo-row" style="--bit-Spacer-large-size:6rem">
                <div class="demo-box">Large, re-sized</div>
                <BitSpacer Size="BitSize.Large" />
                <div class="demo-box">End</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
    <DemoExample Title="Style & Class" RazorCode="@example10RazorCode" Id="example10">
        <div class="example-desc">
            <b>Style</b> and <b>Class</b> land on the spacer element. The space is a margin, so the element has no main-axis size until given one, and it stretches along the cross axis so a decoration shows up in a centered row. Inline styles win over the generated ones.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <div class="demo-row">
                <div class="demo-box">Style</div>
                <BitSpacer Style="min-width:4rem;background:tomato" />
                <div class="demo-box">Class</div>
                <BitSpacer Class="custom-class" />
                <div class="demo-box">End</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
    <DemoExample Title="RTL" RazorCode="@example11RazorCode" Id="example11">
        <div class="example-desc">
            The fixed space is a logical inline-start margin, so it mirrors with the text direction. <b>Dir</b> on a single spacer moves its space to its other side (the tinted spacers below). A flexible spacer behaves the same either way.
        </div>
        <div class="example-content">
            <div class="demo-row" dir="rtl">
                <div class="demo-box">شروع</div>
                <BitSpacer Width="64" />
                <div class="demo-box">میانه</div>
                <BitSpacer />
                <div class="demo-box">پایان</div>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="demo-row">
                <div class="demo-box">Start</div>
                <BitSpacer Width="64" Class="visible-spacer" />
                <div class="demo-box">Middle</div>
            </div>
            <br />
            <div class="demo-row">
                <div class="demo-box">Start</div>
                <BitSpacer Width="64" Class="visible-spacer" Dir="BitDir.Rtl" />
                <div class="demo-box">Middle</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </DemoExample>
</DemoPage>
